Integer coefficients for the element routines in this addon.


Base functions (shape functions)
--------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions


Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss


Procedure pointers
------------------

Summary of assignment of user functions/subroutines to procedure pointers:

  coefficients%func => func
  coefficients%vfunc => vfunc

  coefficients%func1(1)%p => dTemp   ! scalar function for temperature change

  coefficients%set_ninti_user => set_ninti_user
  coefficients%set_Gauss_integration_user => set_Gauss_integration_user

NOTES:

 1) The user functions/subroutines only need to be assigned if they are
    actually being used. If a required assignment is forgotten an error message
    with be given.
 2) The user is free to choose the functions/subroutines for assignment as
    long as the interface is correct.


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   displacement interpolation (base function) 
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral interpolation also the polynomial order p
    needs to be set with coefficients%i(51).

2   pressure interpolation (base function) for mixed (u,p) elements.
    NOTE: if set to 0, standard elements are used (displacements only).
    NOTE: if set to >0, see the stokes elements for LBB compatible or
          stabilized elements. 
    NOTE: for quadrilateral/hexahedron and interpolation=2 (Q2/P1
          Crouzeix-Raviart) see also coefficients%i(62).
    NOTE: for interpolation=14, also the polynomial order p needs to be set with
          coefficients%i(52).

3   For 2D problems (Cartesian, coorsys=0): 0: plane strain, 1: plane stress.

4   0: compressible, 1: incompressible.
    NOTE: This only applies to 2D plane strain and 3D problems. 
    NOTE: mixed (u,p) elements are required for the incompressible assumption.

5   0: No thermal expansion. 1: Include thermal expansion.

6   physqdisp: physical quantity number of the displacement

7   physqpress: physical quantity number of the pressure

8   funcnr: function number of the change in temperature (coefficients%i(2)=1)

9   The change in temperature for thermal expansion is
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(4)
    1: given by a scalar function, funcnr=coefficient%i(8)
       NOTE: scalar function must be assigned to coefficients%func1(1)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(1)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)

10  numerical integration rule (intrule) for the internal domain integrals
    of elements.

11  numerical integration rule (intrule) for boundary integrals for curves,
    surfaces, volumes. For example with intrule=3 (and coefficients%i(40)=0) a
    three-point Gauss-Legendre integration is used for curves and a 3x3
    Gauss-Legendre integration is used for surfaces consisting of quads.

12

13  component choice parameter in computing a derivative.
    See the sources for the possibilities in various derivative routines.

14  vfuncnr for the body force
    <=0: no body force
     >0: function number in the call to vfunc
    NOTE: function vfunc must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc

15  vfuncnr for the traction force
    <=0: no traction force
     >0: function number in the call to vfunc
    NOTE: function vfunc must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc

16  funcnr for the traction force in one the coordinate directions given by
    direction=coefficients%i(17)
    <=0: no traction force
     >0: function number in the call to func
    NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func

17  the direction of the traction force for funcnr=coefficients%i(16)
    1: x-direction
    2: y-direction
    3: z-direction

18-22 

23  coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar (x,y)
    1: Cylindrical (z,r), axisymmetrical
    Note that for cylindrical/axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r),
    i.e. the first coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second
    coordinate is the radial coordinate r. The third coordinate is theta,
    but is not needed explicitly in an axisymmetrical problem.

24-31 

32  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(10) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(10) to each subdomain.

33  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    boundary integrals:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(11) to the complete element domain. 
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(11) to each subdomain.

34  Number of degrees of freedom in the element in one direction (ndf) of the
    displacement interpolation is different from the number of nodes in an
    element.
    0: no
    1: yes. This is tricky stuff, but it might work. The problem is
       isoparametric mapping of the elements, where it is assumed that the
       shape functions of the mapping are identical to the displacement shape
       functions. At the moment it is assumed that the first 1:ndf nodes are
       the nodes of the displacement unknowns, in that order. In this way it is
       possible to use elements with an additional internal node without a
       displacement unknown, provided that it has the highest nodal number.
       A more general mechanism for mixing different shape functions/element
       types is planned but is not available yet.

35  interpolation for the geometrical shape of elements in an object

36  

37  Numerical integration rule and number of subdomains for the
    volume integrals of elements:
     0: the same for all elements as given by coefficients%i(10) and
        coefficients%i(32)
     1: given by the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)
     2: given by user subroutines

38  Layer number. Needs to be specified if the elements are going to be used
    (build,derive etc) for a single layer only.
     0: default: all degrees
     >0: layer number. 

39

40  Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle, hexahedron->pyramid).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)
    NOTE: this affects all numerical integration of interior and boundary
    integrals.

41

51  Polynomial order p of the high-order or spectral interpolation for
    displacement

52  Polynomial order p of the spectral interpolation for pressure

53  pressure projection space interpolation for the stabilized elements
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to be
    set with coefficients%i(54).

54  Polynomial order p of the spectral interpolation for the pressure projection
    space

59  factor for the (optional) diagonal term in the area tensor:
    0: no diagonal term, thus tensor=\int nn dA (=area tensor).
    1: 1/ndim, thus tensor=\int (nn - 1/ndim I) dA
    2: 1, thus tensor=\int (nn - I) dA

62  Mapped or global interpolation for the pressure:
    0: mapped interpolation
    1: global interpolation (scaled shape functions)
    2: global interpolation (unscaled a+b*x+c*y+d*z)
    This is only for discontinuous pressure elements on quadrilaterals and
    hexahedra (such a Q2/P1). Only the global interpolation for the pressure
    (coefficients%i(62)=1) gives optimal convergence rates for general meshes.
    However, for meshes containing rectangles, parallelograms (and 3D
    equivalents) and meshes obtained from regular refinement are fine. See the
    userguide for refs.

63  vfuncnr for exact displacement field (used in linear_elastic_L2_error_norm)
    <=0: exact displacement field = 0
     >0: function number in the call to vfunc
    NOTE: function vfunc must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc

64  funcnr for exact pressure field (used in linear_elastic_L2_error_norm)
    <=0: exact pressure field = 0
     >0: function number in the call to func
    NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func

65  The normal traction (in element linear_elastic_natboun_normal)
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(24)
    1: given by a vector function, funcnr=coefficient%i(66)
       NOTE: function func must be assigned to coefficients%func

66  funcnr for normal traction

67  Use a 3D displacement vector in a 2D space:
      0: no 
      1: yes
    NOTE: the definition of the third displacement component depends on the
          coordinate system used (coefficients%i(23)):
     * displacement component u_z in the 2D Cartesian system
       (coefficients%i(23)=0).
     * circumferential displacement component u_theta in the axisymmetrical
       case, i.e. add "torsion". (coefficients%i(23)=1).
    NOTE: Not all elements have been modified to take this into account.
          An error message is given if incompatible elements are called.  
    NOTE: Not all add-ons have implemented the third component. Hence, check
          the elements before using coefficients%i(67)=1.

68--74

75  secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for the internal domain
    integrals of elements. This is only relevant for prisms and pyramids

76  secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for boundary integrals.
    This is only relevant for volumes consisting of prisms or pyramids.

77  Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(77) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

78--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

